You are an AI coding agent setting up a new application with the VibeWarden
security sidecar. Follow each step in order. Do not skip steps or combine them.
These instructions are generated by vibew {{.VibewVersion}} and match that exact binary.

## Step 1 — Install vibew

  curl -fsSL https://vibewarden.dev/install.sh | sh

Verify: `vibew --version` should print a version string.

## Step 2 — Scaffold the project

  mkdir {{.Name}} && cd {{.Name}}
  vibew init --name {{.Name}} --describe "{{.Describe}}"

This creates vibewarden.yaml, .gitignore, AGENTS-VIBEWARDEN.md, and AGENTS.md.
Read AGENTS-VIBEWARDEN.md — it lists security features you must NOT reimplement.

## Step 3 — Add TLS

  vibew add tls --domain {{.Domain}}

## Step 4 — Write your application

Write your application code. Read AGENTS-VIBEWARDEN.md before writing any auth,
rate-limiting, or security logic — VibeWarden handles those at the sidecar layer.

## Step 5 — Start the dev loop

  vibew dev

The sidecar starts on localhost. Your app is proxied through it automatically.

## Step 6 — Build the deploy bundle

When your application is ready to deploy:

  vibew bundle

This produces a self-contained deploy artifact under .vibewarden/bundle/.

## Step 7 — Deploy to your server

# NOTE: this is the canonical deploy recipe from the bundle README contract.
# Source of truth: internal/app/bundle. Run these commands directly — no shell script wrapper.

  ssh user@{{.Domain}} 'mkdir -p /opt/{{.Name}}'
  scp -r .vibewarden/bundle/* user@{{.Domain}}:/opt/{{.Name}}/
  ssh user@{{.Domain}} "cd /opt/{{.Name}} && docker load -i image.tar && docker compose up -d"

## Step 8 — Verify deployment

  curl -fsSL https://{{.Domain}}/_vibewarden/health

A 200 response confirms TLS, the sidecar, and your app are all healthy.
